WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.323 --> 00:00:03.909
- Really toxic people, I avoid.

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:05.646
I treat film like the military.

00:00:05.646 --> 00:00:10.381
I got out to Hollywood and discovered

00:00:10.381 --> 00:00:12.750
an incredible lack of money.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.675
You are all going to die.

00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:17.669
- Hello, Believe Nation!

00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:18.840
I'm Evan Carmichael.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.184
My one word is believe.

00:00:20.184 --> 00:00:22.216
And I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to

00:00:22.216 --> 00:00:24.784
solve all of the world's major problems.

00:00:24.784 --> 00:00:25.923
So to help on your journey,

00:00:25.923 --> 00:00:28.251
today we're going to learn
from director Joss Whedon

00:00:28.251 --> 00:00:31.335
and my take on his top
ten rules for success.

00:00:31.335 --> 00:00:33.049
Rule number two is my personal favorite,

00:00:33.049 --> 00:00:35.539
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:37.128
And as always, as you're listening,

00:00:37.128 --> 00:00:38.832
if something really resonates with you,

00:00:38.832 --> 00:00:41.087
there's a message that
really holds true to you,

00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:42.738
please leave it down the comments below

00:00:42.738 --> 00:00:45.887
and put it in quotes so that
other people can be inspired.

00:00:45.887 --> 00:00:46.977
And when you write it down,

00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:50.259
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself as well.

00:00:50.259 --> 00:00:51.092
Enjoy.

00:01:04.031 --> 00:01:06.009
- Do you like actors?

00:01:06.009 --> 00:01:07.551
(audience laughing)

00:01:07.551 --> 00:01:09.134
Generally speaking?

00:01:10.993 --> 00:01:12.374
(laughing)

00:01:12.374 --> 00:01:14.719
I'll just, I'll be back
in a couple of minutes.

00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:18.219
- The weird thing is--
- La la la la la la.

00:01:19.809 --> 00:01:23.143
- As many of my best friends
are actors as writers.

00:01:23.143 --> 00:01:25.962
I love actors, and I did not think...

00:01:25.962 --> 00:01:27.351
I had served...

00:01:27.351 --> 00:01:31.490
Again, in the old school, it's
an adversarial relationship.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.661
And I've worked with my share of divas.

00:01:33.661 --> 00:01:38.122
But, I also have enormous
respect for divas.

00:01:38.122 --> 00:01:40.779
They're usually people who
do something extraordinary,

00:01:40.779 --> 00:01:42.842
and know it, and show up and do it.

00:01:42.842 --> 00:01:44.809
And you have to deal with
a lot of other stuff.

00:01:44.809 --> 00:01:46.084
But they usually come in and say,

00:01:46.084 --> 00:01:48.658
you're going to have to deal
with a lot of other stuff.

00:01:48.658 --> 00:01:49.589
I'm a diva.

00:01:49.589 --> 00:01:50.725
So here we go.

00:01:50.725 --> 00:01:52.438
(audience laughing)

00:01:52.438 --> 00:01:53.892
And then you get this beautiful work,

00:01:53.892 --> 00:01:55.133
and you're like, okay,

00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:57.917
thanks for the heads up.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:02:01.535
Really toxic people, I avoid.

00:02:01.535 --> 00:02:03.831
I cast for sanity.

00:02:03.831 --> 00:02:06.215
That's a very important thing to me.

00:02:06.215 --> 00:02:08.717
But toxic people are different than divas.

00:02:08.717 --> 00:02:10.971
Divas are complicated,

00:02:10.971 --> 00:02:12.708
but they know that there is a simplicity

00:02:12.708 --> 00:02:16.925
to what they're going to give
you that you need and want.

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:18.851
Truly toxic people are just about

00:02:18.851 --> 00:02:20.905
trying to tear something down.

00:02:20.905 --> 00:02:23.009
Whether it's somebody else in the thing.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:24.321
Whether it's the story.

00:02:24.321 --> 00:02:25.988
They're about power.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:30.061
Those people have no business in my life,

00:02:30.061 --> 00:02:32.148
and, as far as I'm
concerned, in the industry.

00:02:32.148 --> 00:02:36.315
As a filmmaker, you have to...

00:02:37.205 --> 00:02:39.390
It's sort of the bad teacher thing.

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:41.096
You have to be sure.

00:02:41.096 --> 00:02:44.263
When you're working for somebody else,

00:02:45.386 --> 00:02:46.355
that's it, they're the boss.

00:02:46.355 --> 00:02:49.082
I treat film like the military.

00:02:49.082 --> 00:02:49.915
I always have.

00:02:49.915 --> 00:02:50.748
Film and television.

00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:52.434
When I'm working for someone,

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:53.273
and they're telling...

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:55.275
If they're Henry Fonda in Fort Apache

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:56.730
and they're making all
the wrong decisions,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.306
I listen to them.

00:02:58.306 --> 00:03:00.316
Because someday, I'm going to take over,

00:03:00.316 --> 00:03:02.270
and I'm going to expect the same thing.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.298
And it's not that I don't want dissent

00:03:04.298 --> 00:03:07.773
or I don't want a creative collaboration,

00:03:07.773 --> 00:03:09.092
that's very exciting for me.

00:03:09.092 --> 00:03:12.182
But you have to be in
charge, know what you want,

00:03:12.182 --> 00:03:15.477
and know that you have
the ability to get that.

00:03:15.477 --> 00:03:19.789
And requires a lot of
fierceness and a lot of belief.

00:03:19.789 --> 00:03:23.726
More belief than other people
are going to have in you.

00:03:23.726 --> 00:03:24.559
When I shot...

00:03:24.559 --> 00:03:27.606
The first thing I ever shot
was a pilot presentation

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:30.158
for Buffy, and it was a nightmare.

00:03:30.158 --> 00:03:32.837
None of the crew understood
what I was trying to do,

00:03:32.837 --> 00:03:35.093
or thought I could do it.

00:03:35.093 --> 00:03:37.581
And there was a point when
I just wanted to fold.

00:03:37.581 --> 00:03:38.981
I was just...

00:03:38.981 --> 00:03:40.901
Can I please go home now?

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:41.929
And be a writer.

00:03:41.929 --> 00:03:43.862
But I was miserable just being a writer

00:03:43.862 --> 00:03:46.181
because I wasn't creating the thing whole

00:03:46.181 --> 00:03:47.413
that I wanted to.

00:03:47.413 --> 00:03:49.262
And at some point in the shoot,

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:51.095
I just said to myself,

00:03:51.941 --> 00:03:53.229
this is it, this is your pep talk.

00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:54.879
You're either going to go home

00:03:54.879 --> 00:03:56.004
and regret it for the rest of your life,

00:03:56.004 --> 00:03:59.422
or you're going to go in
there and run this thing.

00:03:59.422 --> 00:04:01.358
And I never looked back.

00:04:01.358 --> 00:04:04.159
- Your first work, it
was a television job.

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:06.662
I think it's Roseanne, is that correct?

00:04:06.662 --> 00:04:07.734
- [Joss] Yeah, that was my first job.

00:04:07.734 --> 00:04:08.894
- So was that,

00:04:08.894 --> 00:04:10.278
did you always want to do television?

00:04:10.278 --> 00:04:12.086
I know you come from a television family.

00:04:12.086 --> 00:04:14.495
Or did you go out to
Hollywood to work in film

00:04:14.495 --> 00:04:15.328
or television?

00:04:15.328 --> 00:04:16.552
How did that work?

00:04:16.552 --> 00:04:19.568
- I would never do television.

00:04:19.568 --> 00:04:20.761
That was beneath me.

00:04:20.761 --> 00:04:22.430
(audience laughing)

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:23.681
I studied film.

00:04:23.681 --> 00:04:25.549
(audience laughing)

00:04:25.549 --> 00:04:27.934
- And my best friend senior
year used to taunt me.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.184
He's like, you're going
to be a third generation

00:04:29.184 --> 00:04:30.038
television writer.

00:04:30.038 --> 00:04:32.354
I am not! Quit it.

00:04:32.354 --> 00:04:33.569
3G TV.

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:35.616
(audience laughing)

00:04:35.616 --> 00:04:37.536
He called me that all the time.

00:04:37.536 --> 00:04:41.883
And then I, I got out to Hollywood

00:04:41.883 --> 00:04:45.614
and discovered an
incredible lack of money.

00:04:45.614 --> 00:04:47.415
(audience laughing)

00:04:47.415 --> 00:04:49.143
- So I sort of started
poking my nose around,

00:04:49.143 --> 00:04:50.912
and realized, oh, there's
some very good television

00:04:50.912 --> 00:04:51.790
that's being made.

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.791
And as soon as I started writing it,

00:04:53.791 --> 00:04:56.247
it was really the first
writing I'd ever done.

00:04:56.247 --> 00:04:57.230
Other than my television specs

00:04:57.230 --> 00:05:00.465
that I wrote trying to get a job.

00:05:00.465 --> 00:05:02.093
That's when I fell in love with writing,

00:05:02.093 --> 00:05:03.772
was through television.

00:05:03.772 --> 00:05:05.637
And I had the privilege
to work on Roseanne,

00:05:05.637 --> 00:05:08.918
which was the best show on.

00:05:08.918 --> 00:05:12.076
Really, it had a level of
reality that no other show

00:05:12.076 --> 00:05:14.427
was really busting out just then.

00:05:14.427 --> 00:05:18.417
- Was it difficult to push
storyline like Willow and Tara's

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:21.000
lesbian love on TV at the time?

00:05:21.836 --> 00:05:24.101
- No, we did it very gradually.

00:05:24.101 --> 00:05:26.260
I'm not sure they knew
that that's what it was.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:27.260
(audience laughing)

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:28.444
For sometime.

00:05:28.444 --> 00:05:31.572
Boy, that seems like a very
intense spell they're casting.

00:05:31.572 --> 00:05:36.196
I'm like, yes!
(audience laughing)

00:05:36.196 --> 00:05:40.029
Sorry, my voice is so
loud in my ear that I...

00:05:41.755 --> 00:05:46.058
Again, I've always sort of
approached things with the idea

00:05:46.058 --> 00:05:48.977
that whoever is giving me
all that money to put it on

00:05:48.977 --> 00:05:50.210
is my partner.

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.744
And that I'm not working against them.

00:05:52.744 --> 00:05:55.312
I'm trying to work with them.

00:05:55.312 --> 00:05:56.360
I'm trying to find the space between

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.328
my artistic expression,
their commercial needs.

00:05:59.328 --> 00:06:01.279
And my artistic expression is commercial.

00:06:01.279 --> 00:06:02.959
I want to make things that people like.

00:06:02.959 --> 00:06:06.915
I'm not wearing a beret and
sitting in a garret smoking.

00:06:06.915 --> 00:06:09.546
(audience laughing)

00:06:09.546 --> 00:06:11.623
'Cause it makes my stomach hurt.

00:06:11.623 --> 00:06:13.040
But, I understand

00:06:15.272 --> 00:06:17.626
what they need and what they want.

00:06:17.626 --> 00:06:18.459
And I like that.

00:06:18.459 --> 00:06:20.954
And I also think with audiences...

00:06:20.954 --> 00:06:22.339
I used to say, we could of made Buffy

00:06:22.339 --> 00:06:23.284
the lesbian separatist,

00:06:23.284 --> 00:06:25.204
but let's start with vampire slayer

00:06:25.204 --> 00:06:29.247
and let's see where it turns.
(audience laughing)

00:06:29.247 --> 00:06:31.495
You always want to sort of get people

00:06:31.495 --> 00:06:33.655
at the edge of their comfort level.

00:06:33.655 --> 00:06:34.488
There are a lot of things that I do

00:06:34.488 --> 00:06:37.342
that people are like,
whoa, that's pretty edgy.

00:06:37.342 --> 00:06:39.092
I'm like, really?

00:06:39.092 --> 00:06:39.925
I don't...

00:06:39.925 --> 00:06:43.030
It surprises me that
it's not more mainstream.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.181
Let's just say
hypothetically that two roads

00:06:45.181 --> 00:06:47.228
diverged in a wood.

00:06:47.228 --> 00:06:50.436
And you took the path less traveled.

00:06:50.436 --> 00:06:53.774
Part of you is just
going, look at that path!

00:06:53.774 --> 00:06:55.941
Over there is much better.

00:06:56.935 --> 00:07:00.179
Everybody's traveling
on it, and it's paved.

00:07:00.179 --> 00:07:03.512
There's like a Starbucks every 50 yards.

00:07:04.664 --> 00:07:06.643
This is wrong.

00:07:06.643 --> 00:07:10.810
On this one, there's nettles
and Robert Frost's body and...

00:07:11.667 --> 00:07:13.795
Somebody should have moved that, I think.

00:07:13.795 --> 00:07:15.267
This feels weird.

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:18.850
And not only is your
mind telling you this,

00:07:19.832 --> 00:07:22.104
it is on that other path,

00:07:22.104 --> 00:07:26.567
it is behaving as though
it is on that path.

00:07:26.567 --> 00:07:29.836
It is doing the opposite
of what you are doing.

00:07:29.836 --> 00:07:32.053
And for your entire life,

00:07:32.053 --> 00:07:34.084
you will be doing, on some level,

00:07:34.084 --> 00:07:36.549
the opposite, not only
of what you are doing,

00:07:36.549 --> 00:07:39.053
but of what you think are.

00:07:39.053 --> 00:07:41.640
That is just going to go on.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:43.406
What you do with all your heart,

00:07:43.406 --> 00:07:45.469
you will do the opposite of.

00:07:45.469 --> 00:07:49.348
And what you need to do

00:07:49.348 --> 00:07:52.545
is to honor that.

00:07:52.545 --> 00:07:53.537
To understand it.

00:07:53.537 --> 00:07:55.176
To unearth it.

00:07:55.176 --> 00:07:59.343
To listen to this other voice.

00:08:00.234 --> 00:08:01.514
All right, I'm losing this now.

00:08:01.514 --> 00:08:03.865
Otherwise it's coming
off in a dramatic fashion

00:08:03.865 --> 00:08:05.365
at the wrong time.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:11.565
I know, I worked on this
hair for a long time, so.

00:08:11.565 --> 00:08:13.315
I had to bust it out.

00:08:15.541 --> 00:08:19.275
You have, which is a rare thing,

00:08:19.275 --> 00:08:22.235
the ability and the responsibility

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:25.902
to listen to the dissent in yourself.

00:08:26.744 --> 00:08:29.137
To at least give it the floor.

00:08:29.137 --> 00:08:30.220
Okay, because

00:08:31.312 --> 00:08:34.080
it is the key, not only to consciousness,

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.191
but to real growth.

00:08:36.191 --> 00:08:40.981
To accept duality is to earn identity.

00:08:40.981 --> 00:08:45.265
And identity is something that
you are constantly earning.

00:08:45.265 --> 00:08:47.600
It is not just who you are.

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.338
It is a process that
you must be active in.

00:08:51.338 --> 00:08:54.855
And it's not parroting your parents

00:08:54.855 --> 00:08:58.361
or even the thoughts of
your learned teachers.

00:08:58.361 --> 00:09:01.094
It is now, more than ever,

00:09:01.094 --> 00:09:03.277
about understanding yourself

00:09:03.277 --> 00:09:04.878
so you can become yourself.

00:09:04.878 --> 00:09:08.101
And I talk about this
contradiction and this tension,

00:09:08.101 --> 00:09:09.759
there's two thing I want to say about it.

00:09:09.759 --> 00:09:13.107
One, it never goes away.

00:09:13.107 --> 00:09:15.322
And if you think that achieving something,

00:09:15.322 --> 00:09:17.271
if you think that solving something,

00:09:17.271 --> 00:09:20.818
if you think a career or a relationship

00:09:20.818 --> 00:09:24.007
will quiet that voice,

00:09:24.007 --> 00:09:25.352
it will not.

00:09:25.352 --> 00:09:29.519
If you think that happiness
means total peace,

00:09:30.818 --> 00:09:32.443
you will never be happy.

00:09:32.443 --> 00:09:35.490
Peace comes from the
acceptance of the part of you

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:38.326
that can never be at peace,

00:09:38.326 --> 00:09:40.870
that will always be in conflict.

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:44.966
If you accept that,
everything gets a lot better.

00:09:44.966 --> 00:09:46.549
The other reason is

00:09:47.622 --> 00:09:50.039
because you are
establishing your identities

00:09:50.039 --> 00:09:51.672
and your beliefs,

00:09:51.672 --> 00:09:53.648
you need to argue yourself down

00:09:53.648 --> 00:09:55.616
because somebody else will.

00:09:55.616 --> 00:09:57.839
Somebody's going to come at you,

00:09:57.839 --> 00:10:02.020
and whatever your belief,
your idea, your ambition,

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:03.308
somebody's going to question it.

00:10:03.308 --> 00:10:05.475
And unless you have first,

00:10:06.644 --> 00:10:08.309
you won't be able to answer back.

00:10:08.309 --> 00:10:10.997
You won't be able to hold your ground.

00:10:10.997 --> 00:10:15.052
You don't believe me, try
taking a stand on just one leg.

00:10:15.052 --> 00:10:17.302
You need to see both sides.

00:10:20.316 --> 00:10:22.336
Because I never studied writing,

00:10:22.336 --> 00:10:25.481
I just sort of had to learn on the job.

00:10:25.481 --> 00:10:28.320
My entire family, my mother, my father,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.074
everyone around them,
teachers and writers.

00:10:32.074 --> 00:10:33.958
Very erudite, very articulate people.

00:10:33.958 --> 00:10:38.119
It was kind of hard for me
to sort of find a voice.

00:10:38.119 --> 00:10:41.501
But it took me just training on the job,

00:10:41.501 --> 00:10:44.468
working relentlessly
since the day I started,

00:10:44.468 --> 00:10:47.278
to sort of discover

00:10:47.278 --> 00:10:49.107
what it was that I was doing

00:10:49.107 --> 00:10:51.547
and sort of discover the
structure behind the voices

00:10:51.547 --> 00:10:52.990
that I was creating.

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:54.674
- How did you settle on Much Ado?

00:10:54.674 --> 00:10:57.973
Is it your favorite Shakespeare
play, or is it just...

00:10:57.973 --> 00:10:58.806
- It's up there.

00:10:58.806 --> 00:11:00.149
It's not my very favorite.

00:11:00.149 --> 00:11:03.772
But it is, it's one we
always talked about doing

00:11:03.772 --> 00:11:07.957
because we knew Amy and
Alexis should definitely

00:11:07.957 --> 00:11:10.040
be Beatrice and Benedick.

00:11:11.465 --> 00:11:13.632
And it's all in one place.

00:11:15.289 --> 00:11:17.786
It's sort of low stress.

00:11:17.786 --> 00:11:20.001
But for years, I just didn't
have a take on the material.

00:11:20.001 --> 00:11:23.934
And then, finally, when I realized I did,

00:11:23.934 --> 00:11:26.509
I said, okay, it's go time.

00:11:26.509 --> 00:11:27.498
And that really,

00:11:27.498 --> 00:11:30.436
that movie exists because
of years and years

00:11:30.436 --> 00:11:34.085
of working with actors
that I absolutely knew

00:11:34.085 --> 00:11:36.645
could do that as though
it were live theater.

00:11:36.645 --> 00:11:39.599
Could walk on set and never get it wrong.

00:11:39.599 --> 00:11:43.766
Because the schedule was
exactly the same as a TV show.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.893
No, actually it was worse.

00:11:46.893 --> 00:11:51.060
It was actually tighter than
a regular network TV show.

00:11:53.171 --> 00:11:54.589
Obviously the language slightly different.

00:11:54.589 --> 00:11:56.895
And no stage directions practically.

00:11:56.895 --> 00:12:00.014
So it was like a (expletive)
ton of work everyday.

00:12:00.014 --> 00:12:01.851
You can only do that when you know

00:12:01.851 --> 00:12:03.901
you have an ensemble who is that is just

00:12:03.901 --> 00:12:04.853
strength to strength.

00:12:04.853 --> 00:12:07.398
So it's really them.

00:12:07.398 --> 00:12:09.316
They're my secret.

00:12:09.316 --> 00:12:11.316
I mean, mine, my secret.

00:12:15.652 --> 00:12:18.313
The pressure isn't greater
if you have characters

00:12:18.313 --> 00:12:22.068
that already exist or, in
the case of the Avengers,

00:12:22.068 --> 00:12:26.272
some of them had been cast
and had their own films.

00:12:26.272 --> 00:12:28.488
Some of them were beloved from comics

00:12:28.488 --> 00:12:31.610
but we were reinventing
them for the films.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.589
Meeting everybody's
expectations really just means

00:12:34.589 --> 00:12:35.913
meeting your own.

00:12:35.913 --> 00:12:39.081
It means that you understand these voices,

00:12:39.081 --> 00:12:41.578
and that the people that
you're making the film for

00:12:41.578 --> 00:12:43.274
hear them the same way you do.

00:12:43.274 --> 00:12:45.244
The people at Marvel are very clear

00:12:45.244 --> 00:12:48.106
that when you make a film of a comic book

00:12:48.106 --> 00:12:50.777
or when you make a film with
a character from another film,

00:12:50.777 --> 00:12:53.689
you cannot do exactly
what was done before.

00:12:53.689 --> 00:12:55.209
You have to translate it

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:56.420
to your own film.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.241
You do it in their style,

00:12:58.241 --> 00:13:01.835
in their sort of house style of Marvel,

00:13:01.835 --> 00:13:03.292
which I grew up with.

00:13:03.292 --> 00:13:05.780
And I feel I understand pretty well.

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:08.293
But you have to make it personal.

00:13:08.293 --> 00:13:11.683
You have to translate
it to a new audience,

00:13:11.683 --> 00:13:13.644
to somebody who hasn't seen or read

00:13:13.644 --> 00:13:15.065
or any of the other things,

00:13:15.065 --> 00:13:17.108
while keeping the essence of the thing.

00:13:17.108 --> 00:13:18.909
Somebody will always argue.

00:13:18.909 --> 00:13:20.260
That's the way you do it.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.310
But nobody can argue with
my passion for doing it.

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:25.533
And I think that's what the
people are really looking for,

00:13:25.533 --> 00:13:27.004
more than the specifics of

00:13:27.004 --> 00:13:29.141
well, in the comics, his
costumes has epaulets up here.

00:13:29.141 --> 00:13:31.165
Why didn't you blah blah blah?

00:13:31.165 --> 00:13:34.415
- You had a really wonderful two years.

00:13:36.434 --> 00:13:37.394
You've had a --

00:13:37.394 --> 00:13:38.227
- It's over, is what you're saying.

00:13:38.227 --> 00:13:39.185
- No, no, no, no!

00:13:39.185 --> 00:13:40.711
- You had a good run,
thank you for coming.

00:13:40.711 --> 00:13:44.065
- How do you feel about
where you are right now?

00:13:44.065 --> 00:13:44.898
Are you--

00:13:44.898 --> 00:13:45.738
- That it's over.

00:13:45.738 --> 00:13:47.618
- Yeah, that's how we always feel, right?

00:13:47.618 --> 00:13:48.451
- Yeah.

00:13:49.518 --> 00:13:51.759
This is a bad one.

00:13:51.759 --> 00:13:56.227
I mean, I know that you get
that after any project probably.

00:13:56.227 --> 00:13:57.780
- It's over.

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:00.082
The jig is up.

00:14:00.082 --> 00:14:01.563
They're going to know.

00:14:01.563 --> 00:14:03.122
(audience laughing)

00:14:03.122 --> 00:14:03.955
- Yep.

00:14:05.492 --> 00:14:07.242
It's worse this time.

00:14:08.374 --> 00:14:10.948
Partially because of the Ultron thing,

00:14:10.948 --> 00:14:12.043
which was difficult.

00:14:12.043 --> 00:14:12.876
And I...

00:14:14.839 --> 00:14:17.932
I should just say, because I feel like,

00:14:17.932 --> 00:14:19.765
I feel like part of...

00:14:20.808 --> 00:14:23.625
Ultron, I am very proud of.

00:14:23.625 --> 00:14:26.537
There were things that did
not meet my expectations

00:14:26.537 --> 00:14:27.954
of myself.

00:14:30.637 --> 00:14:32.881
Then I was so beaten down by the process.

00:14:32.881 --> 00:14:34.338
Some of that was conflict with Marvel,

00:14:34.338 --> 00:14:36.745
which is inevitable.

00:14:36.745 --> 00:14:39.463
But a lot of it was about my own work.

00:14:39.463 --> 00:14:44.130
And then, and I was also exhausted.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:46.991
And then we went right
away and did publicity.

00:14:46.991 --> 00:14:50.199
I sort of created the narrative wherein

00:14:50.199 --> 00:14:52.370
I have not quite accomplished it.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.587
And people just ran with that.

00:14:53.587 --> 00:14:56.758
So it became, well, it's okay.

00:14:56.758 --> 00:15:01.288
It could be better, but
it's not Joss' fault.

00:15:01.288 --> 00:15:04.182
And I think that did a
disservice to the movie

00:15:04.182 --> 00:15:05.772
and to the studio and to myself.

00:15:05.772 --> 00:15:09.272
Ultimately, it was not the right way to be

00:15:11.594 --> 00:15:13.777
because I am very proud of it.

00:15:13.777 --> 00:15:17.944
The things about it that are
wrong frustrate me enormously.

00:15:18.924 --> 00:15:21.797
And I had probably more of those than

00:15:21.797 --> 00:15:24.434
the other movies I've made.

00:15:24.434 --> 00:15:26.927
But, I also got to make,
for the second time,

00:15:26.927 --> 00:15:30.292
an absurdly personal movie
where I got to talk about

00:15:30.292 --> 00:15:34.291
how I felt about humanity
and what it means

00:15:34.291 --> 00:15:37.124
in very esoteric and bizarre ways.

00:15:38.221 --> 00:15:40.902
For hundreds of millions of dollars.

00:15:40.902 --> 00:15:43.094
The fact that Marvel gave
me that opportunity twice

00:15:43.094 --> 00:15:46.255
is so bonkers and so beautiful.

00:15:46.255 --> 00:15:49.278
And the fact that I come
off of it feeling like

00:15:49.278 --> 00:15:53.185
a miserable failure is also bonkers,

00:15:53.185 --> 00:15:55.199
but not in a cute way.

00:15:55.199 --> 00:15:57.230
It becomes problematic.

00:15:57.230 --> 00:16:00.563
And i don't know what it's like for you.

00:16:02.368 --> 00:16:06.451
I took my first vacation,
ever, after that movie.

00:16:07.453 --> 00:16:10.589
And I don't mean ever,
but my first vacation

00:16:10.589 --> 00:16:13.440
that was more than two weeks in 25 years

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:16.465
except for one 4 month vacation

00:16:16.465 --> 00:16:18.480
where I wrote the Buffy musical.

00:16:18.480 --> 00:16:20.256
(audience cheering)

00:16:20.256 --> 00:16:21.089
That was lonely.

00:16:21.089 --> 00:16:24.216
And so, I really set out to do nothing,

00:16:24.216 --> 00:16:25.049
to accomplish nothing.

00:16:25.049 --> 00:16:26.285
And I'm proud to say

00:16:26.285 --> 00:16:28.453
that I have truly accomplished nothing.

00:16:28.453 --> 00:16:29.286
- Did you get there?

00:16:29.286 --> 00:16:30.119
- Yeah, I really,

00:16:30.119 --> 00:16:34.435
I got to a higher state
of (expletive) it all.

00:16:34.435 --> 00:16:37.228
- For you, that's a major departure.

00:16:37.228 --> 00:16:38.061
- I'm proud.

00:16:38.061 --> 00:16:39.311
And I'm also...

00:16:41.186 --> 00:16:44.958
But my resting state is not a good one.

00:16:44.958 --> 00:16:46.863
- You work hard.

00:16:46.863 --> 00:16:47.696
- I work hard.

00:16:47.696 --> 00:16:48.529
I love to work.

00:16:48.529 --> 00:16:49.519
I love the work that I do.

00:16:49.519 --> 00:16:51.564
And I'm very happy and grateful about it.

00:16:51.564 --> 00:16:55.219
When I'm not doing it, I
remember that I hate myself.

00:16:55.219 --> 00:16:58.491
So it's not good so much to be...

00:16:58.491 --> 00:16:59.979
So the vacation's over.

00:16:59.979 --> 00:17:02.830
Now I'm writing again, and so things--

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:03.663
- Are you back at it?

00:17:03.663 --> 00:17:05.387
- Do you feel--
- Oh yeah.

00:17:05.387 --> 00:17:08.011
When I was sitting where
you guys are sitting,

00:17:08.011 --> 00:17:10.261
the speaker was Bill Cosby.

00:17:11.408 --> 00:17:12.451
Funny man Bill Cosby.

00:17:12.451 --> 00:17:14.287
He was very funny, and he was very brief,

00:17:14.287 --> 00:17:16.768
and I think him for that.

00:17:16.768 --> 00:17:20.613
But, he gave us a message

00:17:20.613 --> 00:17:22.628
that I really took with me,

00:17:22.628 --> 00:17:25.800
that a lot of us never forgot.

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:27.883
About changing the world.

00:17:28.743 --> 00:17:32.855
He said, you're not going
to change the world,

00:17:32.855 --> 00:17:33.938
so don't try.

00:17:36.221 --> 00:17:37.847
That was it.

00:17:37.847 --> 00:17:40.430
He didn't buy that back at all.

00:17:41.785 --> 00:17:44.289
And then he complained about
buying his daughter a car,

00:17:44.289 --> 00:17:45.289
and we left.

00:17:48.637 --> 00:17:52.142
And I remember thinking,
I think I could do better.

00:17:52.142 --> 00:17:56.642
I think maybe I could be a
little more inspiring than that.

00:17:56.642 --> 00:17:59.836
And so, what I'd like

00:17:59.836 --> 00:18:02.586
to say to all of you

00:18:04.534 --> 00:18:09.232
is that you are all going to die.

00:18:09.232 --> 00:18:12.967
(audience laughing)

00:18:12.967 --> 00:18:15.910
Okay, so, this is a
good commencement speech

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:19.432
'cause I'm figuring it's
got to go up from here.

00:18:19.432 --> 00:18:22.790
It can only get better, so this is good.

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:25.181
I mean, it can't get more depressing.

00:18:25.181 --> 00:18:29.104
You have, in fact, already begun to die.

00:18:29.104 --> 00:18:30.700
'Kay.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:31.950
You look great.

00:18:33.704 --> 00:18:36.037
Don't get me wrong.

00:18:36.037 --> 00:18:37.069
And you are youth and beauty.

00:18:37.069 --> 00:18:38.581
You are at your physical peak.

00:18:38.581 --> 00:18:42.414
Your bodies have just
gotten off the ski slope

00:18:43.307 --> 00:18:46.604
on the peak of growth and potential.

00:18:46.604 --> 00:18:50.771
And now comes the black
diamond mogul run to the grave.

00:18:53.949 --> 00:18:55.648
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:18:55.648 --> 00:18:59.900
I made this video because
TellingItLikeItIs2000 asked me to.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:01.134
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:19:01.134 --> 00:19:03.484
in the next top 10, check out the link

00:19:03.484 --> 00:19:06.771
in the description, and
go and cast your vote.

00:19:06.771 --> 00:19:07.604
I'd also love to know

00:19:07.604 --> 00:19:09.272
what was your favorite
message from this video?

00:19:09.272 --> 00:19:10.630
What did you learn from this video

00:19:10.630 --> 00:19:12.798
that you're going to immediately apply

00:19:12.798 --> 00:19:14.993
somehow in your life or in your business?

00:19:14.993 --> 00:19:16.276
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:19:16.276 --> 00:19:17.449
I'm really curious to find out.

00:19:17.449 --> 00:19:19.731
I also want to give a quick
shout to Oskar Andermo.

00:19:19.731 --> 00:19:21.388
Oskar, thank you so much for picking up

00:19:21.388 --> 00:19:23.523
a copy of my book, Your One Word,

00:19:23.523 --> 00:19:25.164
doing a review on your YouTube channel,

00:19:25.164 --> 00:19:27.868
and posting it on your
Twitter account as well.

00:19:27.868 --> 00:19:30.445
I really, really, really
appreciate the support, man.

00:19:30.445 --> 00:19:32.244
And I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:19:32.244 --> 00:19:36.286
- Today's book report is the
One Word by Evan Carmichael.

00:19:36.286 --> 00:19:37.636
- So thank you guys again for watching.

00:19:37.636 --> 00:19:38.924
I believe in you.

00:19:38.924 --> 00:19:40.787
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:19:40.787 --> 00:19:42.924
and whatever your one word is.

00:19:42.924 --> 00:19:45.341
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:19:51.634 --> 00:19:53.155
- [Audience member] If
there's a story you like,

00:19:53.155 --> 00:19:54.539
it doesn't have to be Shakespeare,

00:19:54.539 --> 00:19:55.420
that you haven't written,

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.171
it's a universe you haven't created,

00:19:57.171 --> 00:19:59.524
what about it has to be
artistically fulfilling

00:19:59.524 --> 00:20:01.618
or challenging for you to pursue it?

00:20:01.618 --> 00:20:02.984
Because for me, what was surprising

00:20:02.984 --> 00:20:04.049
about you doing Shakespeare

00:20:04.049 --> 00:20:05.857
wasn't that it's not sci-fi or fantasy,

00:20:05.857 --> 00:20:09.700
it's that you, for the
first time in a long time,

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:12.332
worked on a project you hadn't written,

00:20:12.332 --> 00:20:14.843
and it wasn't a universe
that you had created.

00:20:14.843 --> 00:20:18.260
- Yeah, really the only things I directed

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:20.819
that I hadn't written at all

00:20:20.819 --> 00:20:23.475
were episodes of the Office and Glee

00:20:23.475 --> 00:20:24.987
because I loved those shows,

00:20:24.987 --> 00:20:28.692
and I knew the people who were doing them.

00:20:28.692 --> 00:20:30.626
I was going to say I had free time,

00:20:30.626 --> 00:20:34.793
but I'm pretty sure, I mean,
my career was on the skids.

00:20:39.361 --> 00:20:41.513
Something like the
Avengers, it's been cast,

00:20:41.513 --> 00:20:44.930
there's a whole huge history that I know.

00:20:48.214 --> 00:20:49.131
It's all...

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:52.743
If you can't find your own way in,

00:20:52.743 --> 00:20:55.264
then you just don't do it.

00:20:55.264 --> 00:20:56.345
The fact of the matter is,

00:20:56.345 --> 00:20:58.042
because I've been a script doctor,

00:20:58.042 --> 00:20:59.012
before I was a showrunner,

00:20:59.012 --> 00:21:01.812
I was used to coming in where
things were already set.

00:21:01.812 --> 00:21:04.375
After the pilot of a show,

00:21:04.375 --> 00:21:05.998
a lot of the work's been done.

00:21:05.998 --> 00:21:08.494
And you're interpreting
what you've got there.

00:21:08.494 --> 00:21:11.744
When you come in as a script doctor,

00:21:11.744 --> 00:21:13.639
they're like, and then
he goes into a plane.

00:21:13.639 --> 00:21:15.826
I'm like, I have a way better idea

00:21:15.826 --> 00:21:18.800
that we already bought the plane.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:19.633
It's good. I know.

00:21:19.633 --> 00:21:20.466
It's good. I make it work.

00:21:20.466 --> 00:21:21.307
I'm going to work with the plane.

00:21:21.307 --> 00:21:23.469
It's a good, it's a good idea.

00:21:23.469 --> 00:21:26.058
So, you sort of

00:21:26.058 --> 00:21:28.311
you have to find the heart of the thing.

00:21:28.311 --> 00:21:29.565
You find your heart of the thing.

00:21:29.565 --> 00:21:30.873
With Marvel, I said,

00:21:30.873 --> 00:21:32.914
look, I know what you want.

00:21:32.914 --> 00:21:34.265
This is what I want.

00:21:34.265 --> 00:21:35.953
I want to make a war movie.

00:21:35.953 --> 00:21:37.008
I want to make the Dirty Dozen.

00:21:37.008 --> 00:21:37.977
I want to make Blackhawk Down.

00:21:37.977 --> 00:21:40.578
I don't want to make any
squeaky clean, perfect.

00:21:40.578 --> 00:21:42.539
I want to take a toll on these guys.

00:21:42.539 --> 00:21:44.650
And they're like, we get that, we love it.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:46.237
Do it in the mighty Marvel manner,

00:21:46.237 --> 00:21:48.625
and everybody wins.

00:21:48.625 --> 00:21:50.621
So, we knew going in that

00:21:50.621 --> 00:21:53.789
I had my obsessions and that
they fit in that universe

00:21:53.789 --> 00:21:57.594
With this, it's a question
of interpreting his work

00:21:57.594 --> 00:21:59.938
the way I would, which is
in a depressing fashion.

00:21:59.938 --> 00:22:02.441
(audience laughing)

00:22:02.441 --> 00:22:05.620
It's very easy to fall
in love with something.

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:07.869
What? The entire world
is covered with water?

00:22:07.869 --> 00:22:09.670
Include me in.

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:12.437
(audience laughing)

00:22:12.437 --> 00:22:14.165
And yes, you can get heartbroken,

00:22:14.165 --> 00:22:17.187
but you have to start that way.

00:22:17.187 --> 00:22:20.241
The parameters, they always exist.

00:22:20.241 --> 00:22:22.566
If you're doing a genre,
if you're doing...

00:22:22.566 --> 00:22:24.707
It's a comedy. They must laugh.

00:22:24.707 --> 00:22:27.272
Already the page is not totally blank.

00:22:27.272 --> 00:22:29.233
So, how much of it is filled in?

00:22:29.233 --> 00:22:30.696
Whether it's completely written like this.

00:22:30.696 --> 00:22:33.408
Whether it's, where there
are a lot of elements

00:22:33.408 --> 00:22:34.778
that are set, like the Avengers.

00:22:34.778 --> 00:22:36.893
Or whether it's a show that's yours,

00:22:36.893 --> 00:22:39.446
but it's been on for three years.

00:22:39.446 --> 00:22:41.759
You always have to find the new way in.

00:22:41.759 --> 00:22:44.675
- How do you go from

00:22:44.675 --> 00:22:47.370
super-big budget Avengers to this?

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:51.142
Compressed, 12 day shoot in your home.

00:22:51.142 --> 00:22:52.235
- Honestly, there's no difference

00:22:52.235 --> 00:22:55.393
between the big movies and
the little ones for me.

00:22:55.393 --> 00:22:57.664
You're always fighting
everybody's schedule.

00:22:57.664 --> 00:22:59.619
In this case, because
they all had day jobs.

00:22:59.619 --> 00:23:01.940
In the other case, because
they were all famous.

00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:05.016
You're always getting your
set at the last minute.

00:23:05.016 --> 00:23:06.513
You're always behind.

00:23:06.513 --> 00:23:08.042
You're always worried
about the same things.

00:23:08.042 --> 00:23:10.778
You're always trying to
communicate the same things.

00:23:10.778 --> 00:23:11.896
Shooting is shooting.

00:23:11.896 --> 00:23:13.256
Storytelling is storytelling.

00:23:13.256 --> 00:23:15.192
If you're not writing about yourself,

00:23:15.192 --> 00:23:17.086
why are you writing?

00:23:17.086 --> 00:23:19.686
For me, if you're not telling a story...

00:23:19.686 --> 00:23:21.172
Spinning a yarn is fine,

00:23:21.172 --> 00:23:22.597
and there are some people
who are great at it.

00:23:22.597 --> 00:23:24.701
And they're great at things
that I'm not great at.

00:23:24.701 --> 00:23:27.868
Like intricate heist plots and things.

00:23:29.245 --> 00:23:32.255
That I admire and envy, but...

00:23:32.255 --> 00:23:34.890
If you're sitting down to write something

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:37.030
or make something that's
going to take three years

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:38.811
out of your life,

00:23:38.811 --> 00:23:41.659
why would you not want to tell people

00:23:41.659 --> 00:23:44.995
something that's important to you to say?

00:23:44.995 --> 00:23:46.763
I don't mean a moral.

00:23:46.763 --> 00:23:50.151
I just mean an examination
of the human condition.

00:23:50.151 --> 00:23:53.734
You want, you want to
be able to talk about

00:23:54.645 --> 00:23:58.710
the politics of personality.

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:02.866
And that's something it
took me a while to find.

00:24:02.866 --> 00:24:05.578
I started to find it
on Alien Resurrection,

00:24:05.578 --> 00:24:08.758
which is the last thing
I wrote before Buffy,

00:24:08.758 --> 00:24:10.091
before the show.

00:24:11.213 --> 00:24:12.166
That was the first time I went,

00:24:12.166 --> 00:24:15.347
oh, this is a metaphor.

00:24:15.347 --> 00:24:19.639
And the only way this works is
if I feel the way she feels.

00:24:19.639 --> 00:24:21.489
And that was sort of the beginning

00:24:21.489 --> 00:24:23.055
of becoming a storyteller,

00:24:23.055 --> 00:24:25.001
instead of a yarn spinner.

00:24:25.001 --> 00:24:27.501
And to me, if I can't do that,

00:24:28.418 --> 00:24:31.751
if I can't make that connection,

00:24:31.751 --> 00:24:33.634
then I'm wasting people's time.

00:24:33.634 --> 00:24:37.303
And as much as I may look
back at anything I've made

00:24:37.303 --> 00:24:40.168
and go, flawed, flawed,
flawed, flawed, flawed,

00:24:40.168 --> 00:24:41.672
embarrassing, embarrassing,

00:24:41.672 --> 00:24:43.200
I never feel like I did that.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.727
I never feel like, well,
I wasted some of his time.

00:24:45.727 --> 00:24:49.310
That's the biggest brag
I'm going to give you.

